    "items": [
      {
        "id": "def-qft-qed-finite-graph-gauge-data",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Finite-graph U(1) phases for matrix kernels",
        "deps": [],
        "dependency_roles": {},
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "statement": "Let $X$ and $E$ be finite sets, let $s,t:E\\to X$ give the source and target of each directed edge, and assume $X\\ne\\varnothing$. Fix a positive integer $r$, a matrix $T_e\\in M_r(\\mathbb C)$ for each $e\\in E$, and an external link phase $\\zeta_e\\in U(1):=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:|z|=1\\}$. The finite spin space is $\\mathcal H_X:=\\bigoplus_{x\\in X}\\mathbb C^r$, of dimension $d=r|X|$.\n\nFor a real vertex phase $\\theta=(\\theta_x)_{x\\in X}\\in\\mathbb R^X$, define\n$$ \\zeta_e^\\theta:=e^{i(\\theta_{s(e)}-\\theta_{t(e)})}\\zeta_e,\\qquad D_\\theta:=\\operatorname{diag}_{x\\in X}(e^{i\\theta_x}I_r)\\in GL_d(\\mathbb C). $$\nDefine the link-hopping matrix $V(\\zeta)\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ by its $x,y$ block indexed by $x,y\\in X$\n$$ V(\\zeta)_{xy}:=\\sum_{\\substack{e\\in E\\\\s(e)=x,\\ t(e)=y}}T_e\\zeta_e . $$\nAn empty sum is the zero $r\\times r$ matrix. Additional fixed diagonal blocks belong to a separate base matrix. These data are finite-dimensional algebraic data; the phases are external parameters, not rotor multiplication operators on $L^2(\\mathbb T^E)$.\n\nThe graph action uses the dimensionless phase angle $\\theta$ as an independent external parameter. It does not choose an embedding of $G=(X,E,s,t)$ into spacetime or assert a continuum limit.",
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, §§6.1.1 and 6.3.1",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S6.html",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted Chapter 6 HTML; local U(1) phase transformation and charged-fermion covariant derivative."
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, §§67–68",
              "url": "https://www.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 616-page author-hosted draft; QED current and Ward-identity conventions, printed pp. 400–404."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "def-qft-qed-finite-grassmann-algebra",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Finite Grassmann algebra and top-coefficient integral",
        "deps": [],
        "dependency_roles": {},
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "statement": "Let $R$ be a commutative unital $\\mathbb C$-algebra and let $d\\ge1$. Let $\\Lambda_d(R)$ be the exterior algebra over $R$ on $2d$ distinct generators $\\psi_1,\\ldots,\\psi_d,\\bar\\psi_1,\\ldots,\\bar\\psi_d$. Thus every pair of generators anticommutes, each generator squares to zero, and coefficients from $R$ commute with every generator. The bar is part of a generator's name; no complex-conjugation relation is imposed.\n\nOrder the generators as $\\bar\\psi_1,\\ldots,\\bar\\psi_d,\\psi_1,\\ldots,\\psi_d$. The corresponding ordered monomials form the $R$-basis of $\\Lambda_d(R)$. Let\n$$ M_d:=\\bar\\psi_1\\cdots\\bar\\psi_d\\psi_1\\cdots\\psi_d $$\nand let $[M_d]P\\in R$ denote the coefficient of $M_d$ in $P\\in\\Lambda_d(R)$. Define the finite Grassmann integral by the $R$-linear functional\n$$ \\mathcal I_d(P):=(-1)^{d(d+1)/2}[M_d]P . $$\nFor $K\\in M_d(R)$, put $B_K:=\\sum_{i,j=1}^d\\bar\\psi_iK_{ij}\\psi_j$. Since every monomial of degree greater than $2d$ vanishes, its exponential is the finite sum\n$$ \\exp(-B_K):=\\sum_{n=0}^{d}\\frac{(-B_K)^n}{n!}. $$\nThe sign factor in $\\mathcal I_d$ is the chosen normalization. Its Gaussian determinant identity, including the value at $K=I_d$, is proved separately.",
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, §44, Formal Development of Fermionic Path Integrals",
              "url": "https://www.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted 616-page draft; Grassmann generators, coefficient integral, change of variables, and finite complex Gaussian determinant, printed pp. 276–281."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "def-qft-qed-photon-energy-multiplier",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Free transverse-photon energy multiplier",
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-photon-transverse-space",
          "def-countable-choice",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-for-real-and-complex-inner-product-spaces"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-photon-transverse-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-for-real-and-complex-inner-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Use the transverse space $\\mathcal H_T=\\operatorname{Ran}M_{P_T}$ of [[def-qft-photon-transverse-space]], with $P_T(\\mathbf k)=I_3-\\mathbf n\\mathbf n^T$ for $\\mathbf k\\ne0$ and any assigned value at the null point $\\mathbf k=0$. In the QFT length convention, assign $\\mathbf k$ units m$^{-1}$ and define the nonnegative measurable multiplier\n$$ \\omega(\\mathbf k):=|\\mathbf k|. $$\nIts maximal multiplication operator on $\\mathcal H_T$ is\n$$ (M_\\omega f)(\\mathbf k):=\\omega(\\mathbf k)f(\\mathbf k),\\qquad D(M_\\omega):=\\{f\\in\\mathcal H_T:\\omega f\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^3,d^3\\mathbf k;\\mathbb C^3)\\}. $$\nBecause $\\omega$ is a scalar multiplier, $\\mathbf k\\cdot f(\\mathbf k)=0$ a.e. implies $\\mathbf k\\cdot(M_\\omega f)(\\mathbf k)=0$ a.e.; thus the displayed multiplication maps its stated domain into $\\mathcal H_T$. The value $\\omega(0)=0$ is immaterial to the $L^2$ operator. For $\\hbar,c>0$, the corresponding one-particle energy convention is $E_1:=\\hbar c M_\\omega$, in joules.",
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 6, §6.2.1",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S6.html",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted HTML Chapter 6; Coulomb-gauge transverse one-particle photon modes and the free-photon energy multiplier."
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, §55, Electrodynamics in Coulomb Gauge",
              "url": "https://www.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 616-page author-hosted draft; §55, printed pp. 335–338, on the transverse projection, free modes, $\\omega=|\\mathbf k|$, and the photon Hamiltonian."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "def-qft-qed-formal-ring",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Finite matrix formal kernel and inverse series",
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-qed-gauge-data",
          "def-qft-qed-finite-graph-gauge-data"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-qed-gauge-data": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-qed-finite-graph-gauge-data": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "statement": "For a formal indeterminate $z$, let $\\mathbb C\\llbracket z\\rrbracket$ be the commutative ring of sequences $(a_0,a_1,\\ldots)$ written $\\sum_{n\\ge0}a_nz^n$, with coefficientwise addition and Cauchy-product multiplication. No convergence of a numerical series is part of this definition. For $d\\ge1$, matrices over $\\mathbb C\\llbracket z\\rrbracket$ use the usual finite matrix addition and multiplication.\n\nUse the finite graph phase data of [[def-qft-qed-finite-graph-gauge-data]] and the planned QED $U(1)$, Clifford, and natural-unit conventions of [[def-qft-qed-gauge-data]]. Let $K_0(\\zeta)\\in GL_d(\\mathbb C)$ be an invertible base matrix and let $V(\\zeta)\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ be its finite link-hopping matrix. The admissible covariant families satisfy\n$$ K_0(\\zeta^\\alpha)=D_\\alpha K_0(\\zeta)D_\\alpha^{-1},\\qquad V(\\zeta^\\alpha)=D_\\alpha V(\\zeta)D_\\alpha^{-1}. $$\nThe scalar base $K_0=mI_d$, $m\\in\\mathbb C\\setminus\\{0\\}$, is an explicit admissible case. Define\n$$ K(z;\\zeta):=K_0(\\zeta)+zV(\\zeta),\\qquad S(z;\\zeta):=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}(-zK_0(\\zeta)^{-1}V(\\zeta))^nK_0(\\zeta)^{-1}. $$\nThe coefficient of each power $z^n$ in $S$ is one finite matrix. The item defines this formal candidate; the following lemma proves its two-sided inverse and covariance properties. Define $\\det A$ for a finite matrix $A$ over $\\mathbb C\\llbracket z\\rrbracket$ by the Leibniz formula $\\det A=\\sum_{\\sigma\\in S_d}\\operatorname{sgn}(\\sigma)\\prod_{j=1}^d A_{j,\\sigma(j)}$.",
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, §§6.1.1 and 6.4–6.5",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S6.html",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted Chapter 6 HTML; local U(1) gauge convention and finite-dimensional QED propagator/rule symbols."
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, §§44 and 67",
              "url": "https://www.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 616-page author-hosted draft; finite Grassmann Gaussian conventions and QED Ward-identity context, printed pp. 276–281 and 400–401."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-qed-coherent-ir-obstruction",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Logarithmic coherent infrared tail: finite energy, no Fock limit",
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-qed-photon-energy-multiplier",
          "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
          "lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere",
          "lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum",
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface",
          "lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure",
          "thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure",
          "def-complex-exponential",
          "thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension",
          "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-qed-photon-energy-multiplier": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-euclidean-chart-measure-agrees-with-polar-surface-measure": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-polar-coordinates-formula-for-lebesgue-measure": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-complex-exponential": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension": "mathematical-premise",
          "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "statement": "Use the transverse one-particle space $\\mathcal H_T$ and multiplier $\\omega(\\mathbf k)$ of [[def-qft-qed-photon-energy-multiplier]], and form its symmetric Fock space $\\mathcal F_s(\\mathcal H_T)$. Let $H_{\\rm ph}:=\\hbar c\\,d\\Gamma(\\omega)$, with $\\hbar,c>0$ and $d\\Gamma(\\omega)$ acting on the $n$-particle sector as multiplication by $\\sum_{j=1}^n|\\mathbf k_j|$, as in [[lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum]]. For a coherent vector use\n$$ \\epsilon(f):=\\bigoplus_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{f^{\\otimes n}}{\\sqrt{n!}},\\qquad C(f):=e^{-\\|f\\|^2/2}\\epsilon(f), $$\nusing the tensor and direct-sum conventions of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]].\n\nLet $\\kappa>0$. Let $b:S^2\\to\\mathbb C^3$ be measurable with $b\\in L^2(S^2,d\\Omega;\\mathbb C^3)$, $\\mathbf n\\cdot b(\\mathbf n)=0$ for a.e. $\\mathbf n$, and\n$$ C_b:=\\int_{S^2}|b(\\mathbf n)|^2\\,d\\Omega>0. $$\nTake $\\mathbf k,\\kappa,\\epsilon$ in inverse metres and $b$ dimensionless; then $f_\\epsilon$ has momentum-wavefunction units $\\mathrm m^{3/2}$, $C_b$ is dimensionless, and $\\hbar c(\\kappa-\\epsilon)$ has energy units.\nFor $0<\\epsilon<\\kappa$, define $f_\\epsilon(0)=0$ and\n$$ f_\\epsilon(\\mathbf k):=\\mathbf1_{\\{\\epsilon<|\\mathbf k|<\\kappa\\}}|\\mathbf k|^{-3/2}b\\!\\left(\\frac{\\mathbf k}{|\\mathbf k|}\\right). $$\nThen\n$$ \\|f_\\epsilon\\|^2=C_b\\log\\frac{\\kappa}{\\epsilon},\\qquad \\langle C(f_\\epsilon),H_{\\rm ph}C(f_\\epsilon)\\rangle =\\hbar c\\,C_b(\\kappa-\\epsilon). $$\nEach $C(f_\\epsilon)$ is a unit vector in $D(H_{\\rm ph})$. As $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$, $C(f_\\epsilon)\\rightharpoonup0$ weakly, but the family has no strong Fock-space limit. The statement is an exact free transverse-mode construction; it asserts no interacting charged sector, continuum QED limit, or empirical observation.",
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Jan Dereziński, Introduction to Representations of the Canonical Commutation and Anticommutation Relations, §§5.2–5.5 and 6.1–6.2",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/math-ph/0511030",
              "locator": "Complete author lecture-note HTML; full Fock direct sum and second quantization, symmetric Fock space, and bosonic Fock representation of the CCR."
            },
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, §6.2.1",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S6.html",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted Chapter 6 HTML; Coulomb-gauge transverse one-particle modes and the free-photon Hamiltonian."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-qed-formal-matrix-ward",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Finite/formal gauge covariance and matrix Ward identity",
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-qed-formal-ring",
          "def-qft-qed-finite-graph-gauge-data",
          "def-qft-qed-gauge-data"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-qed-formal-ring": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-qed-finite-graph-gauge-data": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-qed-gauge-data": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "statement": "Let $K(z;\\zeta)=K_0(\\zeta)+zV(\\zeta)$ and $S(z;\\zeta)$ be the finite-dimensional formal matrices of [[def-qft-qed-formal-ring]], with $d\\ge1$, $K_0(\\zeta)$ invertible, and both coefficient matrices covariant under the vertex phase angles of [[def-qft-qed-finite-graph-gauge-data]]. Then $S$ is a two-sided inverse of $K$ in $M_d(\\mathbb C\\llbracket z\\rrbracket)$, and\n$$ S(z;\\zeta^\\theta)=D_\\theta S(z;\\zeta)D_\\theta^{-1} $$\ncoefficientwise. For a differentiable phase path $D_s=e^{is\\Theta}$, where the real vertex-angle path is $s\\theta$, the variations satisfy\n$$ \\delta K=i[\\Theta,K],\\qquad \\delta S=i[\\Theta,S]=-S(\\delta K)S . $$\nFor ordinary finite matrices $A,B\\in GL_d(\\mathbb C)$, the corresponding resolvent identity is $B^{-1}(B-A)A^{-1}=A^{-1}-B^{-1}$. These are formal or finite algebraic statements; they assert no convergence of a perturbation series, continuum limit, or loop Ward identity.",
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, §§6.1.1 and 6.4",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S6.html",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted Chapter 6 HTML; gauge transformation and finite QED propagator context."
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, §§67–68",
              "url": "https://www.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted draft; Ward identities in QED, printed pp. 400–404. The local item proves only finite/formal matrix identities."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-qed-tree-ward",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Off-shell free Dirac Ward telescoping and tree currents",
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-dirac-projections",
          "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell",
          "def-countable-choice",
          "lem-qft-qed-formal-matrix-ward"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-dirac-projections": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-scalar-mass-shell": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-qed-formal-matrix-ward": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 5,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "statement": "Use the $(-+++)$ coordinate convention, $h=-\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$, and the Clifford matrices of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]]; the shell parameter $\\mu>0$ has inverse-length units as in [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]. For $p=(p^0,\\mathbf p)\\in\\mathbb R^4$, write\n$$ \\Gamma(p):=\\gamma^0p^0-\\sum_{j=1}^3\\gamma^jp_j,\\qquad K(p):=\\Gamma(p)-\\mu I_4. $$\nWhen $h(p,p)\\ne\\mu^2$, define the finite rational inverse\n$$ S_0(p):=\\frac{\\Gamma(p)+\\mu I_4}{h(p,p)-\\mu^2}. $$\nFor $p,p+k$ with both denominators nonzero,\n$$ S_0(p+k)\\Gamma(k)S_0(p)=S_0(p)-S_0(p+k). $$\nFor any finite chain of off-shell momenta, the corresponding insertion differences telescope with both endpoint terms retained. If $u(p),u(p')$ satisfy $(\\Gamma(p)-\\mu)u(p)=0$, $(\\Gamma(p')-\\mu)u(p')=0$ and $p,p'$ lie on the future shell, then $\\bar u(p')\\Gamma(p-p')u(p)=0$, where $\\bar u=u^\\dagger\\beta$. If $v(q)$ satisfies $(\\Gamma(q)+\\mu)v(q)=0$ on the future shell, then $\\bar v(q)\\Gamma(p+q)u(p)=0$. These are finite rational/tree identities; no Feynman boundary value, singular distribution product, loop theorem, or all-diagram gauge-independence claim is included.",
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, §§6.3.1 and 6.4",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S6.html",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted Chapter 6 HTML; charged Dirac current, free propagator symbols, and explicit tree-level current contractions."
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, §§67–68",
              "url": "https://www.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete 616-page author-hosted draft; QED current Ward identities and exact propagator/vertex relation, printed pp. 400–404. The local result is restricted to rational finite matrices and on-shell tree currents."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-qed-grassmann-determinant",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Finite Grassmann Gaussian and gauge-invariant determinant",
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-qed-formal-ring",
          "def-qft-qed-finite-grassmann-algebra"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-qed-formal-ring": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-qed-finite-grassmann-algebra": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "statement": "Let $R=\\mathbb C\\llbracket z\\rrbracket$, let $d\\ge1$, and let $K\\in M_d(R)$ be a finite matrix as in [[def-qft-qed-formal-ring]]. With $\\mathcal I_d$ the top-coefficient functional of [[def-qft-qed-finite-grassmann-algebra]],\n$$ \\mathcal I_d\\!\\left(\\exp\\!\\left(-\\sum_{i,j=1}^d\\bar\\psi_iK_{ij}\\psi_j\\right)\\right)=\\det K. $$\nIf $D=\\operatorname{diag}(u_1,\\ldots,u_d)$ with each $u_i\\in R^\\times$ and $K'=DKD^{-1}$, then the finite Grassmann integral and determinant are gauge invariant: $\\mathcal I_d(\\exp(-\\bar\\psi K'\\psi))=\\det K'=\\det K$. No infinite-dimensional Grassmann measure, continuum limit, or anomaly claim is made.",
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, §44, Formal Development of Fermionic Path Integrals",
              "url": "https://www.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted 616-page draft; finite Grassmann integration and complex Gaussian determinant, printed pp. 276–281."
            },
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, §§6.3.1 and 6.4",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S6.html",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted Chapter 6 HTML; charged-fermion QED and finite-dimensional fermion propagator context."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-qed-conserved-current-gauge",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Gauge-longitudinal kernel decoupling for explicit tree currents",
        "deps": [
          "lem-qft-qed-tree-ward"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "lem-qft-qed-tree-ward": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 6,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "statement": "Let $p_1,p_1',p_2,p_2'$ be future-shell momenta for the same $\\mu>0$ convention as [[lem-qft-qed-tree-ward]], and suppose $k=p_1-p_1'=p_2'-p_2$. Define\n$$ J_1^\\mu:=\\bar u(p_1')\\gamma^\\mu u(p_1),\\qquad J_2^\\nu:=\\bar u(p_2')\\gamma^\\nu u(p_2), $$\nwith the on-shell Dirac equations in [[lem-qft-qed-tree-ward]]. If a finite rational covariant-gauge kernel changes by $\\Delta D_{\\mu\\nu}(k)=c(k)k_\\mu k_\\nu$, where $c(k)\\in\\mathbb C$ is finite at the chosen $k$, then\n$$ J_1^\\mu\\Delta D_{\\mu\\nu}(k)J_2^\\nu=0. $$\nLikewise, for any conserved tree current $J^\\mu$ of the forms proved in [[lem-qft-qed-tree-ward]], the contraction $\\epsilon_\\mu J^\\mu$ is unchanged by $\\epsilon^\\mu\\mapsto\\epsilon^\\mu+\\alpha k^\\mu$. These statements concern only the displayed finite tree-current contractions; they assert no gauge independence for arbitrary loop diagrams or renormalization schemes.",
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, §6.4",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S6.html",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted Chapter 6 HTML; explicit tree-level cancellation of longitudinal covariant-gauge propagator terms by on-shell Dirac currents."
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, §§67–68",
              "url": "https://www.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf",
              "locator": "Complete author-hosted draft; external-photon Ward identities and exact vertex relation, printed pp. 400–404. The present claim is only finite tree algebra."
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
